; The live installer's SELinux policy: what Fedora's targeted policy denies on
; the installer's own paths and nothing else. The live ISO boots enforcing, its
; files carry the policy's own labels (installer/next/osbuild-selinux), and its
; units and programs have no types of their own: systemd starts the session
; script, GNOME Kiosk, the interface and the root helper from /usr/libexec and
; /usr/bin (bin_t), which Fedora runs as unconfined_service_t. Each rule below
; answers a denial the trials recorded and says which.

; The root helper is the installer: it partitions, makes file systems, mounts,
; runs `bootc install to-filesystem` and labels the target. Fedora has a domain
; for exactly that, install_t (anaconda and initial-setup run in it), and
; systemd enters it by itself when a unit runs a program labelled
; install_exec_t (`type_transition init_t install_exec_t:process install_t`).
; Without this label the helper runs as unconfined_service_t and bootc stops:
; it tries to set a label the loaded policy does not define (the denied
; `capability2 mac_admin` of its `chcon` probe, which only install_t has) and,
; failing that, to enter install_t itself, which unconfined_service_t may not
; ("Failed to enter install_t (running as unconfined_service_t)"). install_t is
; an unconfined domain like unconfined_service_t: this adds no authority to
; anything but the helper, and the interface, the session script and the
; compositor stay as Fedora runs a service without a policy of its own.
(filecon "/usr/libexec/ilex-installer/ilex-installerd" file
    (system_u object_r install_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))

; systemd-repart makes each file system in a child ("(mkfs)") that mounts over
; its own /proc/<pid>/mounts, so that mkfs does not mistake the host's mounts
; for the new file system's. The inode takes the process's own label, and
; Fedora's targeted policy gives unconfined_t the right to mount on its own
; /proc files (`allow unconfined_t unconfined_t:file mounton`), but not its
; unconfined siblings: iso::next_unattended recorded
; `{ mounton } comm="(mkfs)" path="/proc/<pid>/mounts" scontext=install_t
; tcontext=install_t tclass=file`. The same grant, for the same domain.
(allow install_t install_t (file (mounton)))
